IMPORT `wra-definitions`

-- wra2-s58-is-and-work.l4: Welfare Reform Act 2012 (UK) ss 58 to 63, income support (lone parents; claimant commitment) and entitlement to work.
-- Layer 1 (the law, objectively). Part wra2. Host Acts: SSCBA = Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992; WRA2009 = Welfare Reform Act 2009.
-- Source: inputs/ukpga-2012-5.txt (a converter script's plain-text rendering; not the official text).
-- Every answer that depends on regulations, or on a provision the Act only amends, is a MAYBE BOOLEAN whose NOTHING means "points outside the text".
-- s 60 (repeals) is a table of the provisions named; ss 61 to 63 share one test of "entitled to be in employment", written once per section as the Act writes it once per section.

§ `s 58 Entitlement of lone parents to income support etc`

-- src:1136 | (2) In section 3(1), in paragraph (b) of subsection (1A) to be inserted into section 124 of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (lone parents with a child under 7 to be included in regulations as a category of person entitled to income support), for “7” there is substituted “ 5 ”.
@ref s 58(2) WRA2009 3(1) inserting SSCBA 124(1A)(b) (src:1136)
GIVEN `the person is a lone parent, as the Welfare Reform Act 2009 means it` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the age of the person's youngest child in years` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 58(2) SSCBA 124(1A)(b) the person is in the category to be included in regulations as entitled to income support` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the person is a lone parent, as the Welfare Reform Act 2009 means it`
    WHEN JUST TRUE THEN
        BRANCH
            IF `the age of the person's youngest child in years` LESS THAN 5
            THEN JUST TRUE
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the category is "lone parents with a child under 5"; a lone parent whose youngest child is older is not placed in it by this provision, and the provision does not say the parent is excluded
            OTHERWISE NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the category is of lone parents
    WHEN JUST FALSE THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "lone parent" is not defined in the sources
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

§ `s 59 Claimant commitment for income support`

-- src:1141 | (a) in subsection (1), after paragraph (e) there is inserted—
-- src:1142 | (ea) he has accepted a claimant commitment;
-- src:1154 | (6) Regulations may provide that in prescribed circumstances, a claimant is to be treated as having satisfied the condition mentioned in section 124(1)(ea).
@ref s 59(2)(a) SSCBA 124(1)(ea) with 124A(6) (src:1141-1142 src:1154)
GIVEN `the claimant has accepted a claimant commitment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations treat the claimant as having satisfied the condition` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(2)(a) SSCBA 124(1)(ea) the claimant satisfies the condition of having accepted a claimant commitment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the claimant has accepted a claimant commitment`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE CONSIDER `regulations treat the claimant as having satisfied the condition`
            -- SSCBA 124A(6): regulations may treat a claimant as having satisfied the condition in prescribed circumstances
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "he has accepted a claimant commitment" is one of the conditions of entitlement; not accepted, and not treated as satisfied
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the prescribed circumstances are in regulations that are not among the sources
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:1143 | (b) after subsection (1B) there is inserted—
-- src:1144 | (1C) Regulations may prescribe circumstances in which a person may be entitled to income support without having accepted a claimant commitment.
@ref s 59(2)(b) SSCBA 124(1C) (src:1143-1144)
GIVEN `regulations prescribe circumstances in which the person may be entitled without having accepted a claimant commitment, and these are they` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(2)(b) SSCBA 124(1C) the person may be entitled to income support without having accepted a claimant commitment` MEANS
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "Regulations may prescribe circumstances in which a person may be entitled to income support without having accepted a claimant commitment"; they are not among the sources
    `regulations prescribe circumstances in which the person may be entitled without having accepted a claimant commitment, and these are they`

§§ `SSCBA 124A: the claimant commitment`


-- src:1147 | (1) For the purposes of this Part a “claimant commitment” is a record of the claimant's responsibilities in relation to an award of income support.
@ref s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(1) (src:1147)
GIVEN `the award` IS A Benefit
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(1) the award is an award of income support` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the award`
    WHEN `income support` THEN TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "in relation to an award of income support" -- an award of any other benefit is not one
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- src:1147 | (1) For the purposes of this Part a “claimant commitment” is a record of the claimant's responsibilities in relation to an award of income support.
@ref s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(1) (src:1147)
GIVEN `the document is a record of the claimant's responsibilities` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the award the record is in relation to` IS A Benefit
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(1) the document is a claimant commitment` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a “claimant commitment” is a record of the claimant's responsibilities in relation to an award of income support" (a definition; "is", not "means", see NOTES)
        `the document is a record of the claimant's responsibilities`
    AND `s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(1) the award is an award of income support` `the award the record is in relation to`

-- src:1148 | (2) A claimant commitment is to be prepared by the Secretary of State and may be reviewed and updated as the Secretary of State thinks fit.
-- src:1156 | (5) In section 2G of that Act (contracting out), in subsection (1), at the end there is inserted—
-- src:1157 | (d) any function under section 124A of the Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 in relation to a claimant commitment.
@ref s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(2) with SSAA 2G(1)(d) (src:1148 src:1156-1157)
GIVEN `who prepared the claimant commitment` IS AN Authority
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(2) the claimant commitment was prepared by the Secretary of State` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who prepared the claimant commitment`
    WHEN `the Secretary of State` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- s 59(5) inserts 124A functions among those that may be contracted out (SSAA 2G(1)(d))
    WHEN `an authorised person` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): "is to be prepared by the Secretary of State" says nothing of preparation by anyone else
    OTHERWISE NOTHING

-- src:1149 | (3) A claimant commitment is to be in such form as the Secretary of State thinks fit.
@ref s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(3) (src:1149)
GIVEN `the Secretary of State thinks the form fit` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(3) the claimant commitment is in the form the Secretary of State thinks fit` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A claimant commitment is to be in such form as the Secretary of State thinks fit"
    `the Secretary of State thinks the form fit`

-- src:1150 | (4) A claimant commitment is to include—
-- src:1151 | (a) any prescribed information, and
-- src:1152 | (b) any other information the Secretary of State considers it appropriate to include.
@ref s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(4) (src:1150-1152)
GIVEN `it includes the prescribed information` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `it includes any other information the Secretary of State considers appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(4) the claimant commitment includes what the subsection requires` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A claimant commitment is to include ... (b) any other information the Secretary of State considers it appropriate to include"
        IF NOT `it includes any other information the Secretary of State considers appropriate`
        THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE CONSIDER `it includes the prescribed information`
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) any prescribed information"
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): what information is prescribed is left to regulations
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:1153 | (5) For the purposes of section 124 and this section a claimant accepts a claimant commitment if, and only if, the claimant accepts the most up-to-date version of it in such manner as may be prescribed.
@ref s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(5) (src:1153)
GIVEN `the claimant accepted the most up-to-date version of the claimant commitment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the claimant accepted it in the manner prescribed` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 59(3) SSCBA 124A(5) the claimant accepts the claimant commitment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "accepts a claimant commitment if, and only if, the claimant accepts the most up-to-date version of it"
        IF NOT `the claimant accepted the most up-to-date version of the claimant commitment`
        THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE CONSIDER `the claimant accepted it in the manner prescribed`
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if, and only if, ... accepts ... in such manner as may be prescribed" -- accepted, but not in the prescribed manner
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the manner of acceptance is prescribed in regulations that are not among the sources
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

§ `s 60 Claimants dependent on drugs etc`

DECLARE `s 60 provision` IS ONE OF
    `s 60(1) section 17C of the Jobseekers Act 1995`
    `s 60(1) Schedule A1 to the Jobseekers Act 1995`
    `s 60(2) section 15A of the Welfare Reform Act 2007`
    `s 60(2) Schedule 1A to the Welfare Reform Act 2007`
    `s 60(3) section 11 of the Welfare Reform Act 2009`
    `s 60(3) Schedule 3 to the Welfare Reform Act 2009`
    `s 60 some other provision`

-- src:1161 | (1) Section 17C of, and Schedule A1 to, the Jobseekers Act 1995 (persons dependent on drugs etc: jobseekers allowance) are repealed.
-- src:1162 | (2) Section 15A of, and Schedule 1A to, the Welfare Reform Act 2007 (persons dependent on drugs etc: employment and support allowance) are repealed.
-- src:1163 | (3) In the Welfare Reform Act 2009, section 11 and Schedule 3 (which includes provision for review of the provisions repealed by this section) are repealed.
@ref s 60(1)-(3) (src:1161-1163)
GIVEN `the provision` IS A `s 60 provision`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 60 the provision is repealed` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the provision`
    WHEN `s 60(1) section 17C of the Jobseekers Act 1995` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 60(1) Schedule A1 to the Jobseekers Act 1995` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 60(2) section 15A of the Welfare Reform Act 2007` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 60(2) Schedule 1A to the Welfare Reform Act 2007` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 60(3) section 11 of the Welfare Reform Act 2009` THEN JUST TRUE
    WHEN `s 60(3) Schedule 3 to the Welfare Reform Act 2009` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): section 60 repeals the six provisions it names and says nothing of any other
    WHEN `s 60 some other provision` THEN NOTHING

§ `s 61 Entitlement to work: jobseeker's allowance`

-- src:1169 | (3) In that section, after subsection (3) there is inserted—
-- src:1170 | (3A) For the purposes of subsection (2)(za), a person is entitled to be in employment in the United Kingdom if, and only if—
-- src:1171 | (a) the person does not under the Immigration Act 1971 require leave to enter or remain in the United Kingdom, or
-- src:1172 | (b) the person has been granted such leave and—
-- src:1173 | (i) the leave is not invalid,
-- src:1174 | (ii) the leave has not for any reason ceased to have effect, and
-- src:1175 | (iii) the leave is not subject to a condition preventing the person from accepting any employment.
@ref s 61(3) JSA 1(3A) (src:1169-1175)
GIVEN `the person does not under the Immigration Act 1971 require leave to enter or remain in the United Kingdom` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has been granted such leave` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the leave is not invalid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the leave has not for any reason ceased to have effect` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the leave is not subject to a condition preventing the person from accepting any employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 61(3) JSA 1(3A) the person is entitled to be in employment in the United Kingdom` MEANS
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person is entitled to be in employment in the United Kingdom if, and only if" (a) or (b)
        `the person does not under the Immigration Act 1971 require leave to enter or remain in the United Kingdom`
    OR  (    `the person has been granted such leave`
         AND `the leave is not invalid`
         AND `the leave has not for any reason ceased to have effect`
         AND `the leave is not subject to a condition preventing the person from accepting any employment`)

-- src:1167 | (2) In section 1 (jobseeker's allowance), in subsection (2), before paragraph (a) there is inserted—
-- src:1168 | (za) is entitled to be in employment in the United Kingdom;
-- src:1176 | (4) In Schedule 1 (supplementary provisions), after paragraph 8 there is inserted—
-- src:1177 | 8ZA
-- src:1178 | Regulations may prescribe circumstances in which a person may be entitled to a jobseeker's allowance without being entitled to be in employment in the United Kingdom.
@ref s 61(2) JSA 1(2)(za) with Sch 1 para 8ZA (src:1167-1168 src:1176-1178)
GIVEN `the claimant is entitled to be in employment in the United Kingdom` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations prescribe circumstances in which the claimant may be entitled to a jobseeker's allowance without being entitled to be in employment, and these are they` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 61(2) JSA 1(2)(za) the claimant satisfies the condition of being entitled to be in employment in the United Kingdom` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the claimant is entitled to be in employment in the United Kingdom`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE CONSIDER `regulations prescribe circumstances in which the claimant may be entitled to a jobseeker's allowance without being entitled to be in employment, and these are they`
            -- JSA Sch 1 para 8ZA: regulations may prescribe such circumstances
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is entitled to be in employment in the United Kingdom" is a condition of entitlement; not met, and not excused
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the prescribed circumstances are in regulations that are not among the sources
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

