`s 63(8) SSCBA 171ZL(2)(ba) the person satisfies the condition of having been entitled to be in the employment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `at the end of the relevant week he was entitled to be in that employment (statutory adoption pay)`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE CONSIDER `regulations provide that subsection (2)(ba) of section 171ZL in prescribed circumstances does not apply`
            -- SSCBA 171ZL(8)(za): the condition does not apply in the circumstances the regulations provide
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
            -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "at the end of the relevant week he was entitled to be in that employment" is a condition; not met, and the regulations do not disapply it
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the regulations are not among the sources
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- src:1231 | (10) In Schedule 11 (statutory sick pay: circumstances in which periods of entitlement do not arise)—
-- src:1232 | (a) in paragraph 2, at the end there is inserted—
-- src:1233 | (i) the employee is not entitled to be in his employment on the relevant date.
-- src:1234 | (b) at the end there is inserted—
-- src:1235 | 9
-- src:1236 | Paragraph 2(i) above does not apply in prescribed circumstances.
@ref s 63(10) SSCBA Sch 11 paras 2(i) and 9 (src:1231-1236)
GIVEN `the employee is not entitled to be in his employment on the relevant date` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide that paragraph 2(i) does not apply` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 63(10) SSCBA Sch 11 para 2(i) the period of entitlement does not arise because the employee is not entitled to be in the employment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the employee is not entitled to be in his employment on the relevant date`
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): paragraph 2 lists circumstances in which periods of entitlement do not arise; (i) is one of them and does not apply here
        THEN NOTHING
        OTHERWISE CONSIDER `regulations provide that paragraph 2(i) does not apply`
            -- the text says that periods of entitlement do not arise in the circumstances paragraph 2 lists, and paragraph 2(i) is such a circumstance
            WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE
            -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Paragraph 2(i) above does not apply in prescribed circumstances" -- in those circumstances this ground gives no answer
            WHEN JUST TRUE THEN NOTHING
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the prescribed circumstances are in regulations that are not among the sources
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
